{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And they came to him in the second year</b> — of the famine. And even though Joseph said, \"and there are yet five years in which there shall be neither plowing nor harvest\" (Genesis 45:6), since Jacob came to Egypt, blessing came with his footsteps and they began to sow and the famine ended, as we learned in the Tosefta of Sotah (10:3) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 47:19). And thus it is mentioned (Genesis Rabbah 10:9): Rabbi Yosei son of Rabbi Hanina said, the famine lasted two years; when Jacob our father went down to Egypt, the famine ended. And when did it return? In the days of Ezekiel, etc. If so, Joseph's words were not fulfilled according to their plain meaning, and the matter would lead one to suspect him concerning his wisdom.\n\nBut perhaps we may say that the famine persisted in the land of Canaan according to Joseph's words, but in Egypt — Jacob our father went down to the Nile before the eyes of Pharaoh and before the eyes of all Egypt, and all his people saw that the Nile rose to meet him, and they knew that the blessing of YHWH was at the feet of the prophet. And if so, \"Joseph sustained his father and his brothers with bread according to the little ones\" would be in the remaining years of plenty and all the days of his father's life, for even after his father's death he said, \"I will sustain you and your little ones\" (Genesis 50:21).\n\nYet despite all this, I am astonished, for if so, the dream was not true, since they showed him the decree but not the actual outcome that came upon them. And I saw there in the Tosefta of Sotah: Rabbi Yosei said, when Jacob our father died, the famine returned to its former state, etc. And furthermore we learned in the Sifre (Ekev 38): \"And Jacob blessed Pharaoh\" — with what did he bless him? That the two years of famine were held back; even so, they were completed after his death, as it says, \"And now do not fear, I will sustain you.\" Just as the sustenance mentioned there (chapter 45:11) the verse speaks of in years of famine, so too the sustenance mentioned here the verse speaks of in years of famine. Rabbi Shimon says: this is not a sanctification of the Name, that the words of the righteous are fulfilled in their lifetime and are taken away after their death. Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon said: I prefer the words of Rabbi Yosei over the words of my father, for this is a sanctification of the Name — that as long as the righteous are in the world, there is blessing in the world; when they are removed from the world, blessing is removed from the world — end quote. And according to this, the famine completed the five remaining years.\n\nAnd the opinion of Rabbi Abraham is that these were two years after Jacob came to Egypt, and thus he wrote: we find in the derash that the famine was removed in the merit of Jacob; also possible is that there was a famine of three years and it was not like the four that had passed. But his words are not correct at all, for the number of the dream and its interpretation equates all seven years equally, and if it were so, the verse would have mentioned the matter of these years. But according to the plain sense, Joseph gathered all the silver found in the land of Egypt and in the land of Canaan over five years and brought it to Pharaoh, for how is it possible that the silver and the livestock would be exhausted in a single year? Rather, the silver sufficed them for all five years, for so is the way of the world. And because not one new or changed thing occurred during all these years, the verse did not recount them, only \"And Joseph gathered up all the silver,\" etc. And after the silver was exhausted, it recounts that they came to Joseph — and this was in the sixth year — and he gave them bread in exchange for their livestock, only allotting to them by it that they would eat according to their sustenance and not to satiety. And that year in which he had undertaken to sustain them with bread in exchange for all their livestock came to an end — and it is the sixth year — and they came to him in the second year of it, meaning the seventh year, and said to him that he should buy them and their land for the bread he would feed them in this seventh year, and after the land would belong to Pharaoh he would give them seed so that the land would not be desolate, for they knew that the seven years of famine were completed and they would have seed and harvest. And this is the reason for \"And Joseph sustained them with bread according to the little ones\" — that he gave them during the years of famine enough for their need of bread, for \"according to the little ones\" hints at precisely this:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "The verse lemma and the phrase 'in the second year of it' (לה) — Ramban reads 'the second year' of the sixth-year arrangement, i.e., the seventh year of famine, resolving the chronological tension with the midrashic view that Jacob's arrival ended the famine early."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:33.687251648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}